 player just played T7. Previous player played T8. Danger of helping someone else complete a chow or pung.\n\nMoreover, I already used T7 and T8 in a chow. Holding onto them doesn't help unless I can form another meld \u2014 impossible.\n\nBut here's a key point: **Can I win by drawing F3?**\n\nYes! If I draw F3, I can declare a pung (even though not yet declared), add it to my melds, and use a pair (e.g., T7-T7? But I only have one T7).\n\nWait \u2014 problem.\n\nAfter adding F3 pung, my melds become:\n- [W7x3]\n- [B3x3]\n- [T7,T8,T9]\n- [F3,F3,F3]\n\nThen I need a **pair** from remaining two tiles.\n\nRemaining tiles after drawing F3: originally five tiles in hand (before draw): [T7, T8, F2, F3, F3]. After drawing F3 \u2192 six tiles: [T7, T8, F2, F3, F3, F3].\n\nThen I can expose [F3,F3,F3] as a pung \u2192 now hand has [T7, T8, F2] left \u2014 three single tiles \u2192 no pair.\n\nAlternatively, keep F3s concealed until win.\n\nBut to win, I must have exactly one pair.\n\nSo best scenario: I already have **two F3s**. If I keep them as a **pair**, and draw or claim a tile to complete another meld.\n\nWhat fourth meld can I complete?\n\nOptions:\n- Complete a pung of T7? Need two more T7s \u2014 only one unshown \u2192 impossible.\n- Same for T8 \u2192 only one unshown.\n- F2? Only one unshown.\n\nNo possibility to complete a new pung from scratch.\n\nOnly viable way: **use F3 pair as eyes**, and complete a fourth meld from current singles.\n\nBut I have [T7, T8, F2] \u2014 all singles.\n\nCan I form a chow from T7, T8? Missing T6 or T9.\n\nT6: only one unshown \u2192 maybe possible.\n\nBut T6 is rare. And I'd need to draw T6 and then discard F2 \u2192 but then I need to win by drawing T6 \u2192 but then how?\n\nHand: [T7, T8, F2] + draw T6 \u2192 [T6, T7, T8] \u2192 chow \u2192 melds: four melds + pair?\n\nBut where is the pair?\n\nUnless I make [F3, F3] the pair.\n\nAh! Yes.\n\nSo plan:\n- Keep **F3, F3** as **pair (eyes)**\n- Use [T7, T8] + draw **T6** or **T9*